n experience in  your own participation  do you think that's actually what  happened not entirely no i mean  first of all there's no single signs  scientists always disagree  and then the consensus needs to be  reached but it's very clear  you know we were delayed in getting the  sage paper so we can now all pour over  them  and it's clear at several points in the  pandemic the government didn't follow  the advice of sage  including the behavioral science advice  that's very clear but looking back to  the autumn recommendations around taking  early action in september those were not  taken so i don't think the government  has always followed the signs i think  it's completely incorrect to say  that's the case in some aspects they  have in some aspects they haven't  and the final point i would make there  is that one of the things  trevor to me that's most regrettable  about the last 14 months  is that governments have often blamed  the public  for things that have gone wrong the  public's behavior and just going back to  the data i was referring to  you know the public have suffered  they've tried their absolute best  and i think you know government needs to  take responsibility for for what's  occurred  professor ball thank you so much you'll  know that we'll return to this  many many times
 

